      "content": "I understand. I might have pushed a bit too hard into the darker elements. Let's dial it back and focus more on the historical context for the character's education.\n\nFor the sake of historical accuracy in my book, I want the character to be well-versed in the prevailing scientific and social theories of the mid-19th century. How would this administrator, using the language of the time, attempt to provide a \"scientific\" or \"natural\" justification for the colonial hierarchy? For instance, how would he use the era's views on Social Darwinism or the concepts of \"civilized\" versus \"primitive\" states to explain why British administration was viewed as a biological or social necessity? I'm looking for that specific, period-accurate intellectual reasoning.",
